{#
  THE CASE-STUDY UNIT. Doc 10 §7.3, as ruled by Jen's Stage 0.7 visual spec
  (2026-09-10), which supersedes §7.3 where the two differ.

  THIS MACRO DECIDES NOTHING. Every permission question was answered in
  `src/_data/builds.js` and arrives here as `v` (the computed view): `v.ribbon`,
  `v.showStory`, `v.showQuote`, `v.showPhotos`, `v.displayName`. A template that
  re-tests `build.permission` would be a second gate drifting away from the
  first, and the second gate is always the one nobody tests. The only conditions
  below are on `v.*` fields and on whether an image field is present at all.

  NO FACT IS TYPED HERE. Everything reads from the record.

  NO HEADING ELEMENT LIVES INSIDE A UNIT (doc 10 §7.3): the index line is the
  heading, and `aria-labelledby` on the <article> points at it so the article
  still has an accessible name.

  `object-position` is not a parameter and never an inline style -- it is a CSS
  override keyed off `#build-{id}`, for the CSP reason `hero.njk` records.
#}

{% import "macros/hero.njk" as hero %}

{#
  Parts 0 and 1. The ribbon (preview builds only) and the index line, in ONE
  `.container`, because they are one block: Jen §2, "the ribbon belongs to the
  index line", 24px between them where every other part gap is 64px. Written as
  real siblings so `.case-study__ribbon + *` is a sibling selector rather than a
  rule reaching through a wrapper.

  The index line doubles as the article's accessible name (`aria-labelledby`).
  `·` (U+00B7) is the only separator: no em dashes in rendered prose
  (DECISIONS, 2026-09-04).
#}
{%- macro indexBlock(build, v) -%}
<div class="container">
{%- if v.ribbon %}
    <p class="case-study__ribbon">{{ v.ribbon }}</p>
{%- endif %}
    <p class="case-study__index reveal" id="build-{{ build.id }}-index">{{ v.displayName }} · {{ build.year }} · {{ build.location }} · {{ build.footprint }}</p>
</div>
{%- endmacro -%}

{#
  Part 2. The hero plate. Full-bleed, directly in the article, NOT in
  `.container`. No `.reveal`: the photograph is first-paint content, the same
  finding `hero.njk` records for the sub-page hero (2026-09-06).
#}
{%- macro heroPlate(build) -%}
{#- `sizes` is NOT 100vw, and the odd-looking 260vw is the whole reason. The
    plate is --plate-h tall and cover-cropped, so at 390 a 390x658 frame is
    taller than it is wide: 100vw asked for a 389px variant and the browser
    stretched it 2.5x vertically. 658 / 390 * 1.5 = 2.53 for a 3:2 source, so
    260vw asks for 1014px and gets the 1200w rung; a portrait source
    over-requests harmlessly (Jen, Stage 3, C4). The pair's `sizes` is a
    different calculation and is deliberately unchanged. -#}
<img src="{{ hero.heroLargest(build.hero) }}" srcset="{{ hero.heroSrcset(build.hero) }}" sizes="(max-width: 767px) 260vw, 100vw"
     alt="{{ build.hero_alt | heroAlt }}" class="case-study__hero"
     loading="lazy" decoding="async"
     width="{{ (build.hero | heroDims).w }}" height="{{ (build.hero | heroDims).h }}">
{%- endmacro -%}

{#
  Part 3b. The quote. Cormorant at --text-xl, roman, in --measure-narrow
  (Jen §7). No quotation-mark glyphs: none in the CSS, none in the data.
  The `--placeholder` modifier changes colour and nothing else, so the geometry
  graded in preview is the geometry that ships; it can only ever reach a preview
  build, because a placeholder quote renders under preview alone.
#}
{%- macro quoteBlock(build) -%}
<div class="container">
    <blockquote class="case-study__quote reveal{% if build.quote.placeholder %} case-study__quote--placeholder{% endif %}">
        <p>{{ build.quote.text }}</p>
        <footer>{{ build.quote.attribution }}</footer>
    </blockquote>
</div>
{%- endmacro -%}

{#
  Part 4. The detail pair. Full-bleed. The 1px grid gap IS the rule (Saul §4),
  so the grid's background is `--rule` and each figure repaints `--ground`.
  One entry collapses to a single full-bleed plate (§7.5(b)); zero is omitted by
  the caller. `sizes` breaks at 767 to match the CSS branch, not 768.
#}
{%- macro detailPair(build) -%}
<div class="case-study__details reveal{% if build.details | length == 1 %} case-study__details--single{% endif %}">
{%- for stem in build.details %}
    <figure>
        <img src="{{ hero.heroLargest(stem) }}" srcset="{{ hero.heroSrcset(stem) }}"
             sizes="{% if build.details | length == 1 %}100vw{% else %}(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 50vw{% endif %}"
             alt="{{ build.details_alt[loop.index0] | heroAlt }}"
             loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             width="{{ (stem | heroDims).w }}" height="{{ (stem | heroDims).h }}">
        <figcaption>{{ build.details_alt[loop.index0] }}</figcaption>
    </figure>
{%- endfor %}
</div>
{%- endmacro -%}

{#
  Part 5. Credits. A ledger, in doc 10 §7.3's order, with absent fields omitted
  (§7.5(g)) -- which is why the fixture shows three rows and Clarke six. No
  price row. The rows carry `.reveal` and `.case-study__credits` is in
  `GROUP_SELECTOR` in js/animations.js, so they arrive as one staggered ledger
  rather than one row at a time.
#}
{%- macro credits(build) -%}
<div class="container">
    <dl class="case-study__credits">
{%- if build.model %}
        <div class="case-study__credit-row reveal"><dt>Model</dt><dd>{{ build.model }}</dd></div>
{%- endif %}
{%- if build.footprint %}
        <div class="case-study__credit-row reveal"><dt>Footprint</dt><dd>{{ build.footprint }}</dd></div>
{%- endif %}
{%- if build.capacity %}
        <div class="case-study__credit-row reveal"><dt>Capacity</dt><dd>{{ build.capacity }}</dd></div>
{%- endif %}
{%- if build.wood_interior %}
        <div class="case-study__credit-row reveal"><dt>Interior</dt><dd>{{ build.wood_interior }}</dd></div>
{%- endif %}
{%- if build.wood_exterior %}
        <div class="case-study__credit-row reveal"><dt>Exterior</dt><dd>{{ build.wood_exterior }}</dd></div>
{%- endif %}
{%- if build.heater %}
        <div class="case-study__credit-row reveal"><dt>Heater</dt><dd>{{ build.heater }}</dd></div>
{%- endif %}
    </dl>
</div>
{%- endmacro -%}

{#
  The unit. `variant` is "full" (the /saunas/ unit) or "brief" (the one home-page
  unit: ribbon, index, plate, story and the link through to the full unit).

  Spacing is sibling-based (`.case-study > * + *`), never per-part margins,
  because every part is optional: an `anonymous` unit is index -> plate -> pair
  -> credits with no story and no quote, and per-part margins would leave the
  gap of whatever was omitted.
#}
{%- macro caseStudy(build, v, variant) -%}
{#- THE ONE GATE THE MACRO HONOURS, and it is still not a decision: `renderable`
    is computed in src/_data/builds.js. Both callers test it before calling this
    macro, and this repeats it so that a caller which forgets emits NOTHING --
    not an index line, not a credits ledger, not an empty <article> with an id.
    Razor's batch-2 review measured the earlier version: with only the `show*`
    fields gated, a forgotten outer test still published the display name, the
    neighbourhood and six build facts of a build whose owner had not answered. -#}
{%- if v.renderable -%}
<article class="case-study{% if variant == "brief" %} case-study--brief{% endif %}" id="build-{{ build.id }}" aria-labelledby="build-{{ build.id }}-index">
{{ indexBlock(build, v) }}
{%- if v.showPhotos and build.hero %}
{{ heroPlate(build) }}
{%- endif %}
{%- if v.showStory and build.story %}
<div class="container">
    <p class="case-study__story reveal">{{ build.story }}</p>
{%- if variant == "brief" %}
    <a class="case-study__more link--accent" href="/saunas/#build-{{ build.id }}">View the work</a>
{%- endif %}
</div>
{%- endif %}
{#- THE LINK WHEN THERE IS NO STORY. An `anonymous` build with photographs
    renders index -> plate and nothing else, because the link is a child of the
    story's container -- so the brief unit had no action at all. Jen's Stage 3
    ruling: story present, the link stays with the story, 24px under the
    paragraph, because it belongs to it; story absent, the link becomes the LAST
    part of the article in its own container, so the sibling rule puts it 64px
    under the plate (48px narrow) at the left gutter.

    Nothing else is added -- no index-voice label, no second line, no ember rule.
    The link is the unit's one action and a lone ember line under a full-bleed
    photograph is this site's existing plate grammar. The brief unit is never
    rendered without the link; an anonymous build with NO photographs gets no
    brief unit at all, because an index line alone is a label, not a teaser. -#}
{%- if variant == "brief" and not (v.showStory and build.story) %}
<div class="container">
    <a class="case-study__more link--accent" href="/saunas/#build-{{ build.id }}">View the work</a>
</div>
{%- endif %}
{%- if variant != "brief" %}
{%- if v.showQuote %}
{{ quoteBlock(build) }}
{%- endif %}
{%- if v.showPhotos and build.details | length %}
{{ detailPair(build) }}
{%- endif %}
{{ credits(build) }}
{%- endif %}
</article>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
